        System.out.println("Card payment: " + amount);
    }
}

class PaymentService {
    public void process(Payment payment,
                        double amount) {
        payment.pay(amount);
    }
}


Usage:
PaymentService service = new PaymentService();

service.process(new UpiPayment(), 1000);
service.process(new CardPayment(), 2000);


Concepts tested:
- Interface
- Abstraction
- Polymorphism
- Loose coupling
- Open/Closed Principle
Q14. Why do we use encapsulation in a banking application?
We should not allow external classes to directly modify account balance.
class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }
}


The balance is private and can only be modified through controlled methods.
Q15. When would you use inheritance versus composition?
Scenario: An e-commerce application has different types of users.

Inheritance:
class User {
    String name;
}

class Admin extends User {
}

class Customer extends User {
}


Composition:
class Car {
    private Engine engine;
}


- Inheritance represents an IS-A relationship.
- Composition represents a HAS-A relationship.
Prefer composition when behavior should be flexible and independently replaceable.
